Let the HLS demuxer define and substitute playlist variables.

An HLS playlist can define variables with EXT-X-DEFINE and use them as {$name} in its addresses
and in its quoted attribute values (RFC 8216bis, sections 4.3, 4.4.2.3 and 6.3.1). A variable is
defined by NAME and VALUE, by IMPORT from the master playlist that loaded a media playlist, or by
QUERYPARAM from the query of the playlist's own URL, which is how a CDN hands a token from the
master playlist down to every media playlist and segment. The demuxer knew none of it, so such a
stream asked its server for addresses that still held the variable names, and every one failed.

The demuxer now reads EXT-X-DEFINE and replaces each reference in URI lines and in the attribute
lists of the four tags it reads, EXT-X-STREAM-INF, EXT-X-MEDIA, EXT-X-KEY and EXT-X-MAP. A
replacement is not replaced again. QUERYPARAM reads the URL the playlist came from after any
redirect, through the same "location" option that already rebases its relative addresses, and
percent-decodes the value. The master playlist's variables are kept for its media playlists to
import, and every other playlist's live for one load, so a reload defines them afresh.

The specification's failures fail the playlist with AVERROR_INVALIDDATA and an error that names
the variable: a reference to a variable that no earlier EXT-X-DEFINE gave, a tag with other than
exactly one of NAME, IMPORT and QUERYPARAM, a NAME without a VALUE, a name outside [a-zA-Z0-9_-],
a name defined twice, an IMPORT in a playlist that no master playlist loaded or of a name the
master does not define, and a QUERYPARAM that the URL does not carry with a value. A playlist that
uses no reference is read exactly as before.

Applied by BuildFFmpegTask before configure.

